{"id":"fit-learning-rate-and-batch-size-scaling","t":"Fit learning-rate and batch-size scaling laws from sweeps","tags":["Scaling Laws","Training"],"d":"Hard","rt":"numpy","fn":"fit_hparam_scaling","tol":[1e-06,1e-08],"pkgs":[],"st":"The best learning rate and batch size drift as compute grows, and at the final scale you get one shot. The usual remedy: sweep both hyperparameters at several small budgets, find each budget's optimum, and fit power laws $\\eta^\\star \\propto C^{a}$ and $B^\\star \\propto C^{b}$ to extrapolate.\n\nWrite `fit_hparam_scaling(compute, lr, bs, loss, c_target)` and return a dict.\n\n## Inputs\nFloat64 arrays of equal length, one entry per training run: `compute` (FLOPs $C$), `lr`, `bs` (batch size) and final `loss`, in any order. Runs of the same budget have exactly equal `compute`. `c_target` is a float.\n\n## Per budget\nWith $u = \\log_{10}\\eta$ and $v = \\log_{10} B$, fit the quadratic surface\n\n$$\\text{loss} \\approx c_0 + c_1 u + c_2 v + c_3 u^2 + c_4 uv + c_5 v^2$$\n\nby least squares over that budget's runs (use `np.linalg.lstsq` on the design matrix with columns in this order).\n\n- **Drop** the budget if it has fewer than 6 runs, or if the fitted surface has no minimum: keep it only if $c_3 > 0$ and $4 c_3 c_5 - c_4^2 > 0$.\n- Otherwise its optimum $(u^\\star, v^\\star)$ solves the stationarity condition $\\begin{pmatrix} 2c_3 & c_4 \\\\ c_4 & 2c_5 \\end{pmatrix}\\begin{pmatrix} u \\\\ v\\end{pmatrix} = -\\begin{pmatrix} c_1 \\\\ c_2 \\end{pmatrix}$, and `lr_opt` $= 10^{u^\\star}$, `bs_opt` $= 10^{v^\\star}$, `loss_opt` is the surface's value there.\n\n## Across budgets\nFit $\\log_{10}\\eta^\\star$ on $\\log_{10} C$ by OLS with an intercept over the kept budgets, and the same for $\\log_{10} B^\\star$. The slopes are the exponents; evaluate both lines at `c_target`.\n\n## Output\n| key | value |\n|---|---|\n| `\"budgets\"`, `\"lr_opt\"`, `\"bs_opt\"`, `\"loss_opt\"` | float64 arrays over kept budgets, sorted by budget ascending |\n| `\"lr_exponent\"`, `\"bs_exponent\"` | floats |\n| `\"lr_pred\"`, `\"bs_pred\"` | floats, predicted optimum at `c_target` |\n\nIf fewer than 2 budgets are kept, the last four values are `float('nan')`. The tests always keep at least one budget.","starter":"import numpy as np\n\ndef fit_hparam_scaling(compute, lr, bs, loss, c_target):\n    \"\"\"Return a dict with budgets, lr_opt, bs_opt, loss_opt, lr_exponent, bs_exponent, lr_pred, bs_pred.\"\"\"\n    raise NotImplementedError\n","hints":["Work in log space throughout. Group the runs with `np.unique(compute)` (already sorted) and a boolean mask per budget.","Build the six-column design matrix `[1, u, v, u², uv, v²]`, solve with `lstsq`, check the positive-definiteness conditions, then solve the 2×2 linear system for the stationary point.","The exponents are OLS slopes of $u^\\star$ and $v^\\star$ (already base-10 logs) against $\\log_{10} C$. Predict by evaluating each line at $\\log_{10}$ `c_target` and exponentiating with base 10."],"sol":"import numpy as np\n\ndef _line(x, y):\n    \"\"\"OLS slope and intercept of y on x.\"\"\"\n    xc = x - x.mean()\n    slope = (xc @ (y - y.mean())) / (xc @ xc)\n    return slope, y.mean() - slope * x.mean()\n\ndef fit_hparam_scaling(compute, lr, bs, loss, c_target):\n    compute, loss = np.asarray(compute, dtype=float), np.asarray(loss, dtype=float)\n    u_all, v_all = np.log10(lr), np.log10(bs)\n    kept = []                                              # (budget, u*, v*, loss*)\n    for c in np.unique(compute):                           # np.unique is sorted ascending\n        sel = compute == c\n        if sel.sum() < 6:\n            continue\n        u, v = u_all[sel], v_all[sel]\n        X = np.column_stack([np.ones_like(u), u, v, u * u, u * v, v * v])\n        c0, c1, c2, c3, c4, c5 = np.linalg.lstsq(X, loss[sel], rcond=None)[0]\n        if not (c3 > 0 and 4 * c3 * c5 - c4 * c4 > 0):    # Hessian must be positive definite\n            continue\n        us, vs = np.linalg.solve(np.array([[2 * c3, c4], [c4, 2 * c5]]), -np.array([c1, c2]))\n        best = c0 + c1 * us + c2 * vs + c3 * us * us + c4 * us * vs + c5 * vs * vs\n        kept.append((c, us, vs, best))\n\n    budgets, us, vs, best = (np.array(col) for col in zip(*kept))\n    out = {\"budgets\": budgets, \"lr_opt\": 10.0 ** us, \"bs_opt\": 10.0 ** vs, \"loss_opt\": best}\n    nan = float(\"nan\")\n    if len(budgets) < 2:\n        return {**out, \"lr_exponent\": nan, \"bs_exponent\": nan, \"lr_pred\": nan, \"bs_pred\": nan}\n    x, xt = np.log10(budgets), np.log10(c_target)\n    a_lr, b_lr = _line(x, us)\n    a_bs, b_bs = _line(x, vs)\n    return {**out, \"lr_exponent\": float(a_lr), \"bs_exponent\": float(a_bs),\n            \"lr_pred\": float(10.0 ** (a_lr * xt + b_lr)), \"bs_pred\": float(10.0 ** (a_bs * xt + b_bs))}\n","ex_md":"## Why a quadratic in log space\nNear the optimum, loss as a function of $\\log\\eta$ and $\\log B$ is a smooth bowl, and a second-order Taylor expansion is the simplest model that has a minimum. The cross term $c_4$ matters: learning rate and batch size interact (a larger batch tolerates a larger rate), so optimising each separately along grid lines gives the wrong point. The positive-definiteness check rejects sweeps that missed the bowl (test 5's saddle budget) instead of reporting a maximum or saddle point as the optimum.\n\n## Why fit the optimum, not pick the best run\nThe best grid point snaps to the grid, so its drift with compute is a staircase. The fitted optimum interpolates between grid points and averages out noise across all runs of a budget, which gives far more stable exponents.\n\n## Complexity\nPer budget, one $n \\times 6$ least-squares solve and one $2 \\times 2$ solve. Everything else is linear in the number of runs.\n\n## Common bugs\n- Natural logs in one place and base-10 in another: the exponents are base-independent, but the predictions are not.\n- Dropping the $uv$ term, or using the diagonal $2c_3, 2c_5$ alone to find the optimum.\n- Keeping budgets whose surface is not convex.\n- Sorting kept budgets by insertion order rather than by compute.\n\n## Interview follow-ups\n- Published sweeps often find the optimal learning rate nearly flat in compute but the optimal batch size growing. How does that relate to the critical batch size?\n- How would you put error bars on `lr_pred`? (Bootstrap runs within each budget and refit.)","nex":2,"shown":[{"in":"array([1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18])\narray([0.001, 0.01 , 0.1  , 0.001, 0.01 , 0.1  , 0.01 ])\narray([  10.,   10.,   10.,  100.,  100.,  100., 1000.])\narray([2.5, 1.5, 2.5, 2. , 1. , 2. , 1.5])\n1e+20","out":"{'budgets': array([1.e+18]), 'lr_opt': array([0.01]), 'bs_opt': array([100.]), 'loss_opt': array([1.]), 'lr_exponent': nan, 'bs_exponent': nan, 'lr_pred': nan, 'bs_pred': nan}"},{"in":"array([1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+18, 1.e+20,\n       1.e+20, 1.e+20, 1.e+20, 1.e+20, 1.e+20, 1.e+20])\narray([0.001, 0.01 , 0.1  , 0.001, 0.01 , 0.1  , 0.01 , 0.001, 0.01 ,\n       0.1  , 0.001, 0.01 , 0.1  , 0.01 ])\narray([  10.,   10.,   10.,  100.,  100.,  100., 1000.,   10.,   10.,\n         10.,  100.,  100.,  100., 1000.])\narray([3.  , 2.  , 3.  , 2.  , 1.  , 2.  , 2.  , 4.75, 4.75, 6.75, 1.75,\n       1.75, 3.75, 0.75])\n1e+22","out":"{'budgets': array([1.e+18, 1.e+20]), 'lr_opt': array([0.01      , 0.00316228]), 'bs_opt': array([ 100., 1000.]), 'loss_opt': array([1. , 0.5]), 'lr_exponent': -0.2499999999999969, 'bs_exponent': 0.5, 'lr_pred': 0.0010000000000000328, 'bs_pred': 10000.0}"}],"lessons":[{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l04-23","t":"Hyper-Parameter Optimization","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l12-01","t":"Train LLM — Sequence Length vs Batch Size","c":"AI Research"},{"href":"../ai-research-track-web/index.html#l13-05","t":"Scaling laws","c":"AI Research"}],"related":["isoflop-sweep-exponent","power-law-log-fit","estimate-critical-batch-size"],"ntests":7,"tj":"[{\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.01], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 1000.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [2.5, 1.5, 2.5, 2.0, 1.0, 2.0, 1.5], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1e+20], \"e\": {\"__dict__\": [[\"budgets\", {\"__nd__\": [1e+18], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"lr_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [0.00999999999999998], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"bs_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [100.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"loss_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [1.000000000000004], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"lr_exponent\", {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}], [\"bs_exponent\", {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}], [\"lr_pred\", {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}], [\"bs_pred\", {\"__f__\": \"nan\"}]]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+18, 1e+20, 1e+20, 1e+20, 1e+20, 1e+20, 1e+20, 1e+20], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.01, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.001, 0.01, 0.1, 0.01], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 1000.0, 10.0, 10.0, 10.0, 100.0, 100.0, 100.0, 1000.0], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, {\"__nd__\": [3.0, 2.0, 3.0, 2.0, 1.0, 2.0, 2.0, 4.75, 4.75, 6.75, 1.75, 1.75, 3.75, 0.75], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}, 1e+22], \"e\": {\"__dict__\": [[\"budgets\", {\"__nd__\": [1e+18, 1e+20], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"lr_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [0.010000000000000031, 0.0031622776601684345], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"bs_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [99.9999999999999, 999.999999999999], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"loss_opt\", {\"__nd__\": [1.0000000000000089, 0.5000000000000213], \"dtype\": \"float64\"}], [\"lr_exponent\", -0.2499999999999969], [\"bs_exponent\", 0.5], [\"lr_pred\", 0.0010000000000000328], [\"bs_pred\", 10000.0]]}}, {\"a\": [{\"__nd__\": [1e+19, 1e+19, 1e+18, 1e+20, 1e+17, 1e+18, 1e+20, 1e+19, 1e+19, 1e+20, 1e+17, 1e+18, 1e+17, 1e+20, 1e+17,